# Entity Access Policies

[Waaseyaa](https://github.com/waaseyaa) uses access policies to decide who can view, create, update, or delete entity data.

You define the rules in code. The framework evaluates them before it touches storage, so your access logic stays close to the entity types it protects.

## Define an Access Policy

Write a policy class for the entity type you want to protect.

```php
<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace App\Entity\Policy;

use App\Entity\Post;
use Waaseyaa\Auth\User;
use Waaseyaa\Entity\Access\AccessDecision;
use Waaseyaa\Entity\Access\EntityAccessPolicy;

final class PostAccessPolicy implements EntityAccessPolicy
{
    public function view(User $user, Post $post): AccessDecision
    {
        if ($post->isPublished()) {
            return AccessDecision::allow();
        }

        return $user->id() === $post->authorId()
            ? AccessDecision::allow()
            : AccessDecision::deny();
    }

    public function update(User $user, Post $post): AccessDecision
    {
        return $user->id() === $post->authorId()
            ? AccessDecision::allow()
            : AccessDecision::deny();
    }

    public function delete(User $user, Post $post): AccessDecision
    {
        return $user->hasRole('admin')
            ? AccessDecision::allow()
            : AccessDecision::deny();
    }
}
```

This policy allows public reads for published posts, author edits for their own content, and admin deletes. Each method returns an explicit allow or deny decision.

## Attach the Policy to Your Entity Type

Register the policy when you define the entity type.

```php
<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace App\Entity;

use App\Entity\Policy\PostAccessPolicy;
use Waaseyaa\Entity\Attribute\EntityType;

#[EntityType(
    id: 'post',
    label: 'Post',
    policy: PostAccessPolicy::class,
)]
final class Post
{
    // Fields and behavior.
}
```

This tells the framework which policy to load for `post` entities. Every entity operation now passes through that class.

## Check Access Before You Write

Ask the access manager before you update or delete an entity.

```php
<?php

$decision = $access->for($user)->can('update', $post);

if ($decision->isDenied()) {
    throw new ForbiddenHttpException();
}

$post->title = 'Updated title';
$posts->save($post);
```

The access manager evaluates the registered policy and returns a decision object. You can stop the request early and keep unauthorized writes out of your application flow.

## Restrict Individual Fields

You can protect sensitive fields with field-level rules.

```php
<?php

$decision = $access->for($user)->field('salary')->can('view', $employee);

if ($decision->isDenied()) {
    unset($payload['salary']);
}
```

This check lets you expose the rest of the entity while hiding one field. Use it when different parts of the same record need different visibility rules.

## Keep Policy Logic Close to the Model

Put the policy beside the entity type it protects. You can read the rules in one place, test them directly, and change them without scattering permission checks across controllers and services.
